        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.09,
            0.462,
            0.146
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Are electromagnets also used in real life, for lifting objects?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.48,
            0.084,
            0.804,
            0.105
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.1.2 Lifting electromagnets"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.107,
            0.883,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "Lifting electromagnets are strong electromagnets, that may be hung to the cranes. The crane operator can control the"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.162,
            0.884,
            0.275
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "magnet by switching the current ON and OFF. When the current is turned ON, the electromagnet lifts the iron/steel objects; when the current is switched OFF, magnetic field disappears, and the objects are released. Lifting electromagnets are widely used in factories and scrap yards, to move, lift, and sort heavy metal items efficiently."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.294,
            0.437,
            0.315
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.354,
            0.26,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.33,
            0.862,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "We have learnt that when electric current flows through a conductor (like a wire), it produces a magnetic field around it. In the higher grades, you will learn even more about this wonderful link between electricity and magnetism, including the exciting idea that just as electricity can produce magnetism, a moving magnet can also lead to an electric current. This deep connection between electricity and magnetism is vital to our daily lives, as it forms the basis of many devices, from electric motors to power generators."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.495,
            0.852,
            0.521
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.2 Does a Current Carrying Wire Get Hot?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.553,
            0.627,
            0.575
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.5: Let us observe"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.078,
            0.609,
            0.16,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.626,
            0.465,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "While doing the activity for electromagnet, did you also notice that the wire ends got warm? Why would that happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.472,
            0.601,
            0.882,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "In this activity, we will use a special kind of wire, called a nichrome wire."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.642,
            0.884,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "z Take a cardboard piece of about 10 cm length and 10 cm width, two nails, a nichrome wire of thickness about 0.3 mm (26–28 gauge) and length of 10 cm, an electric cell, a cell holder, a switch, and connecting wires."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.757,
            0.8,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "Mount the nails on the cardboard about 5 cm apart."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.778,
            0.883,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": "z Tie the nichrome wire between these nails and make the connections as shown in Fig. 4.5 with the switch in OFF position."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.836,
            0.733,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "z Touch the nichrome wire. What do you feel?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.857,
            0.883,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "z Move the switch to ON position for about 30 s and then move it back to OFF. Touch the nichrome wire momentarily"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
